Currently our permissions are stored something like this:
```js
[{
  name: "foo"
  blah: ..
},
  name: "bar"
  blah: ..
}]
```

This causes us problems periodically because it allows for the same permission to appear more than once. A better data structure would be one that enforces this rule:

```js
{
 foo: { blah: .. },
 bar: { blah: .. }
}
```

There are lots of places now where 401s just result in console errors, and we don't catch them properly until the user does a hard refresh.

This may be a bug in PouchDB, or our use of it, because when there are replication errors `$q` complains about a "Possibly unhandled rejection".

The correct angular thing is to implement this in inbox.js:

```js
  inboxControllers.config(
    function(
      $httpProvider
    ) {
      'ngInject';

      $httpProvider.interceptors.push(function() {
        return {
          'responseError': function(rejection) {
            console.log('RESPONSE ERROR DETECTED', rejection);
            // Then we'd ideally like to
            // Session.navigateToLogin();
          }
        };
      });
  });
```

However, it looks like PouchDB doesn't end up using `$http` as its interface (I would think it would, which is why we care about `pouchdb-angular`, but maybe not), so this doesn't do anything for PouchDB initiated requests (ie 99% of them).

We should either:
 - Work out why our promise chains are broken, fix them, and put rejection handling there (perhaps patch PouchDB to allow for configuring a hook to deal with errors generically)
 - Go Deeper™, and muck around with prototypes. See things like this: https://stackoverflow.com/questions/25335648/how-to-intercept-all-ajax-requests-made-by-different-js-libraries

